solution.statement:
(a) A process in which a system does work on its environment but does not exchange heat with its environment is known as an **adiabatic** process.

(b) We can write the ideal gas law as $PV=Nk_BT$. Since $N$ and $k_B$ are constants, the relevant proportionality relation is $PV\propto T$. If we factor out the constant $PV^\gamma=C$, then we get $V^{1-\gamma}\propto T$. For a spherical shell $V$ scales like $r^2$ (rather than the usual $r^3$ for a solid sphere), so $r^{2(1-\gamma)}\propto T$.

$$
\boxed{T\propto r^{2(1-\gamma)}=r^{-4/3}}
$$

(c) Just plugging in the given numbers to the proportionality relation, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
T&=T_0\left(\frac r{r_0}\right)^{-4/3}=(10000\ ^\circ\mathrm K)\left(\frac{1\mathrm{ly}}{50\mathrm{AU}}\right)^{-4/3}\\
&=\boxed{0.7\ \mathrm K}
\end{aligned}
$$

> **Source note:** The native solution substitution prints 10000 °K while the question uses 10 000 K; the degree sign is retained.
